## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design incremental geometry data structures, reason about dominance, and assess batch-update trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Pareto frontier maintenance
- Batch incremental updates
- Monotone frontier properties (x increasing, y decreasing)
- Trade-offs: update time vs rebuild cost

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode sketch: merge batch into frontier
function insertBatch(frontier, batch){
  batch.sort((a,b)=> a.x-b.x);
  // prune intra-batch dominated
  let filtered=[]; let maxY=-Infinity;
  for(let p of batch){ if(p.y>maxY){ filtered.push(p); maxY=p.y; } }
  // merge with frontier (frontier: sorted by x, y strictly decreasing)
  let i=0,j=0; let newFrontier=[];
  while(i<frontier.length || j<filtered.length){
    let a = frontier[i], b = filtered[j];
    if(!a) { newFrontier.push(...filtered.slice(j)); break; }
    if(!b) { newFrontier.push(...frontier.slice(i)); break; }
    if(a.x < b.x){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; }
    else if(b.x < a.x){ newFrontier.push(b); j++; }
    else { // equal x, keep larger y
      if(a.y > b.y){ newFrontier.push(a); i++; j++; }
      else { newFrontier.push(b); i++; j++; }
    }
  }
  // optional prune to maintain y-decreasing property
  return pruneFrontier(newFrontier);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This problem evaluates understanding of dynamic tree updates and amortized analysis, demonstrating the power of subtree aggregation over naive full recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- Tree DFS preprocessing for subtree computation
- Subtree size tracking for batch updates
- Lazy aggregation techniques
- Amortized cost analysis
- Performance trade-offs vs complete traversal

## Data Structure Details
- **Preprocessing**: O(n) DFS to compute subtree sizes and initial S
- **Per-edge update**: O(1) using δ × size[c] aggregation
- **Per-batch cost**: O(B) where B is batch size
- **Memory**: O(n) for storing subtree sizes and edge weights
- **Optimizations**: (1) Batch processing to reduce constant factors, (2) Cache-friendly array layout
- **Comparison**: DFS recomputation costs O(n) per batch vs O(B) with aggregation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Preprocessing
const adj = [...];
const n = adj.length;
const size = Array(n+1).fill(0);
const w = Array(n+1).fill(0); // w[c] = weight to parent
let S = 0;

function dfs(u, parent) {
    size[u] = 1;
    for (const v of adj[u]) {
        if (v !== parent) {
            dfs(v, u);
            size[u] += size[v];
            S += w[v] * size[v];
        }
    }
}

// Update function
function update(edge, newWeight) {
    const [p, c] = edge;
    const delta = newWeight - w[c];
    w[c] = newWeight;
    S += delta * size[c];
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason about streaming similarity with limited memory, a practical beginner-level challenge that still touches core complexity trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
